      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's consider the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). This is a quadratic expression. To determine if it has any real roots, we use the discriminant \\(\\Delta\\):\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\nwhere \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = 7\\). Plugging these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\n\\]\nSince the discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) is negative, the quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots. Additionally, since the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) (which is \\(a\\)) is positive, the parabola opens upwards. This means that the quadratic expression is always positive for all real values of \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + 2x + 7 > 0 \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in \\mathbb{R}\n\\]\n\nNext, we focus on the numerator \\(x + 6\\). This is a linear expression. We find its roots by setting it equal to zero:\n\\[\nx + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -6\n\\]\nThus, the numerator \\(x + 6\\) is zero at \\(x = -6\\).\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) by determining where the numerator and denominator have the same sign (both positive or both negative).\n\n1. For \\(x > -6\\):\n    - Since \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive, and \\(x + 6\\) is positive (because \\(x > -6\\)), the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is positive.\n\n2. For \\(x < -6\\):\n    - Since \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive, and \\(x + 6\\) is negative (because \\(x < -6\\)), the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is negative.\n\nWe need to consider the intervals where the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) holds. This inequality will be satisfied when the fraction is either positive (from step 1) or zero (from the numerator, since the fraction is zero only where the numerator is zero).\n\nSince the fraction is zero only at \\(x = -6\\), which we already considered separately, the solution to the inequality is:\n\\[\nx \\le -6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x > -6\n\\]\n\nThus, combining these intervals, we get:\n\\[\nx \\in (-\\infty, -6] \\cup (-6, \\infty)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer in interval notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6] \\cup (-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
