      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, we note that the denominator $x^2 + 2x + 7$ is always positive since it is the sum of perfect squares. So, we just need to solve the inequality $\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0$.\nNext, we identify the x-intercepts of the numerator by putting $\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} = 0.$ This simplifies to $x = -6$, so $-6$ is the only solution.\nOne solution can be found by plotting the two functions $y = \\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}$ and $y = 0$ and looking at their intersection points.\n(https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Roots_and_Zeros_of_a_Function#Graphs)\nFinally, we open these two intervals by picking $-6$ as our boundary point to get $x \\le -6$. So, $\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6]}\\cup[0,+\\infty)$. The answer is (\\infty,-6]\\cup[0,+\\infty)",
